The '''Aruanãyára Empire''', officially known as the '''Realm of the Stone Path''' ({{langx|tpn|Itá-Rapé-Retã}}, {{lit|land of the stone path}}), was the largest empire in [[pre-colonial Abyala]]. The administrative, political, and military center of the empire was in the city of [[Takuakesetyba|Takûakesétyba]]. The [[History of the Itarapeta|Proto-Itarapeta culture]] rose from the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén|Southern Matarrañyan]] highlands sometime in the early [[13th century]]. The Burawi explorer [[tba]] was the first Mesogean to reach the Aruanãyára Empire in 1524. Later, in 1547, the [[Colonial History of Burawa|Burawi]] began the conquest of the Aruanãyára, and by 1575 the capital had been captured, and the Empire disintegrated.

From 1422 to 1525, the Aruanãyára incorporated a large portion of southwestern [[Abyala]], centered on the [[Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]] mountain range, using conquest and peaceful assimilation, among other methods. At its largest, the empire joined modern-day [[Asikyira]] with what is now the [[Levante, Balisca|Baliscan Levante]] (extending from [[Luzamontaña]] in the south, to the western and south-central fringes of the [[Alpujarra]]).

Its official language was [[Classical Aruanãyára|Aruanãyára]].

